From 4dc711012cb9de6d6fdb5c7d3ae19c81f690010a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:06:43 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 11/15] fix(streaming): hide text tool calls inside reasoning
 and CLI streams
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

K2 sometimes returns tool_calls=0 and streams
`<tool_call>{...}</tool_call>` as content deltas. The CLI's
ThinkPassthroughFilter had no tool-markup handling at all, so the tags and
the JSON body were printed live to the terminal. The TagFilter `<think>`
passthrough forwarded tool markup inside the reasoning stream verbatim, so
the Telegram and Slack live drafts showed the tags and JSON. A `</tool_call>`
inside a stripped `<think>` block also ended the suppression early and leaked
the reasoning tail plus a literal `</think>`.

Unify the tool-markup stripping in the shared TagFilter: it now tracks the
current region (answer text vs. a streamed `<think>` block) and strips
tool-call tags and their bodies in both, while still forwarding reasoning
text in passthrough/render mode. ThinkPassthroughFilter becomes a thin
wrapper over TagFilter's new `.render` mode, and composeFinalReply strips
tool markup from the quoted reasoning via helpers.stripToolCallMarkup.

Adds regression tests using K2's exact captured content split at every
chunk boundary, for each filter mode and the CLI/Telegram/channel sinks.

Round 3 hardening (review findings):
- A well-formed tool block whose body contained a blank line was released as
  ordinary text by the paragraph-break heuristic before its close tag was
  seen, leaking the open tag and the body (e.g. a multi-line command). The
  body is buffered anyway, so the release now waits for the close tag or the
  buffer cap, in both the streaming TagFilter and helpers.skipToolCallBlock.
- Standalone control tokens (`<tool_calls_section_begin>`, ...) were matched
  exactly, so the same "model omits the >" class that close tags already
  tolerated leaked the whole token. They are now matched as name + `>`/
  whitespace in both the streaming TagFilter and helpers.stripToolCallMarkup.
- helpers.stripToolCallMarkup left a stray close tag (no opener) verbatim
  while the streaming filter swallowed it, so the `Reasoning:` quote in
  composeFinalReply showed markup the stream already hides. It now drops
  stray close tags too.

Round 4 hardening (review findings): one unifying rule replaces the
case-by-case handling of unterminated tag tokens. While consuming any tag
token (open, close, standalone) after its name matched and before its `>`,
a `<` (or end of stream) means the token was unterminated: the partial token
is dropped and the `<` is reprocessed from the current region's normal
state. The same rule is mirrored in helpers.stripToolCallMarkup, so streaming
and batch stay identical. Fixes:
- A close tag that omitted its `>` (whitespace delimiter) right before
  `</think>` entered `.close_tag` and ate up to the `>` of `</think>`, so the
  reasoning region never closed and strip mode dropped the whole answer.
- A standalone token that omitted its `>` before a tool block over-consumed
  up to the `>` of the following `<tool_call>` opener, leaking the body.
- An incomplete close tag `</tool_call` immediately followed by `<` flushed
  the buffer (including the `<`), so a following `</think>` was never
  recognised.
Each fix has a regression test covering all ThinkModes and every 2-way and
3-way chunk split, asserting parity with stripToolCallMarkup.

Round 5 hardening (review findings):
- An *unterminated* provider (DSML/opaque) marker opened inside a `<think>`
  region ran its body to a matching closer or the end of the stream, so a
  malformed marker swallowed the `</think>` terminator and the whole visible
  answer in all three modes. `.opaque_body_lt` now ends the marker (and the
  reasoning region) on `</think>`, exactly like `.tool_lt` does for
  `<tool_call>`; helpers.skipOpaqueMarkers mirrors it with an `in_think`
  flag, and stripToolCallMarkup tracks `<think>` nesting to supply it.
- The tool-body window (4096 bytes from the open tag's `<`) was too small for
  a realistic multi-KiB K2 text call and was counted differently in the two
  implementations, so a well-formed call whose body exceeded ~4084 bytes
  leaked the open tag and the full JSON body in the live stream, with a
  one-byte streaming-vs-batch parity gap at the boundary. The window is now
  16384 bytes and both implementations admit a close tag that begins exactly
  at the boundary (the filter releases only when `body_len > tool_body_max`;
  the batch scanner already admitted offset == the cap).
Each fix has a regression test covering all ThinkModes and every 2-way and
3-way chunk split (a sampled subset for the multi-KiB bodies, where an
exhaustive 3-way sweep would be O(n^2)), asserting parity with
stripToolCallMarkup.
Round 6 hardening (review findings): the end-of-stream "never lose text"
fallback released an *unterminated* tool block verbatim, so the exact 75-byte
K2 sample `<tool_call>\n{json}` (no closing tag) — which the agent's dispatcher
executes — was printed by every surface. The fallback now drops a buffered block
whose payload is a call the dispatcher would execute and releases everything
else, via a predicate that mirrors parseXmlToolCalls' unclosed recovery (the
shared repairJson, then a JSON object with a plausible `name`, or a `name{...}`
prefix). The bracket dialect the dispatcher also accepts (`[TOOL_CALL]` /
`[tool_call]`, marker_len 11) is recognised by the streaming filter and the
batch stripper in all three ThinkModes. repairJson moves to json_util so the
predicate and the dispatcher share one repair.

Round 7 hardening (review findings): the dispatcher's parseXmlToolCalls accepts
the first </X> as a fallback close and matches "tool_call" case-insensitively,
but the display only knew a fixed close-name list, so a block closed by a
mismatched or any-case close tag (</arg_value>, </function>, </TOOL_CALL>,
</Tool_Call>) leaked its tags and JSON into live drafts and into the
non-streamed Reasoning: quote. The close rule is now derived from the
dispatcher's own scan: parseXmlToolCalls and both display mirrors share
providers.helpers.nextToolCallClose, and a dispatcher-opened block ends at the
first close whose name contains "tool_call" (any case) or whose preceding bytes
are a complete call, so a </html> embedded inside a JSON string is not mistaken
for the block's close. Prose and code that merely mention the tags stay
visible. Every input is covered at every 2-way and 3-way chunk split in all
ThinkModes, with batch parity against stripToolCallMarkup.

Round 8 hardening (review findings):
- On marker-dense text (`<tool_call>literal ` repeated to 30–240 KiB),
  stripToolCallMarkup took quadratic time (O(N^2), 20 s at 240 KiB) because its
  main loop called dispatcherBlockReleaseEnd and skipToolCallBlock at every
  unclosed opener, each scanning forward to end-of-string. The forward scans are
  now managed by a CloseScanner that caches the next close-tag position and
  resumes from it, turning the repeated suffix searches into a single linear
  pass.
- A dense run of closed calls (`<tool_call>{...}</tool_call>` repeated) was also
  quadratic (~5 s at 1 MiB) because parseDispatcherXmlToolCalls' marker search
  used std.mem.indexOf on the whole remaining slice for each marker dialect,
  re-scanning the tail for absent dialects every iteration. The marker search
  now uses std.mem.indexOfAnyPos(..., "<[") to find candidate positions and
  advances a single search cursor, so every byte is visited a constant number
  of times.
Each fix has regression tests covering linear execution on large dense streams
and asserting streaming/batch parity at every chunk split.
